\details{
\subsection{Identical records}{

With \code{complete_cases = TRUE} (the default) the data are passed through
\code{\link[=selection_frame]{selection_frame()}} first, so every candidate sees the same rows and the
likelihood-based columns are comparable. Fit the \emph{selected} specification to
the full data afterwards.
}

\subsection{What the table is sorted by, and why you should not rely on it}{

Rows are ordered by \code{cv_deltaNLL} when cross-validation ran and by
\code{deltaAIC} otherwise. Never read the winner off the first row in downstream
code — use \code{\link[=best_candidate]{best_candidate()}}, which looks the value up in the column and
skips rows that are not comparable.
}

\subsection{Comparability}{

The \code{comparable} column is \code{TRUE} only for a candidate that fitted, whose
Hessian was positive definite, that saw the modal number of records, and —
under CV — had every fold resolve and score the modal number of held-out
points. \strong{Compare only those rows}, and do not quote a delta between two
candidates unless both are comparable: a likelihood from a fit that has not
resolved is not a basis for a comparison, and neither is one computed over a
different set of observations.

\code{\link[sdmTMB:sanity]{sdmTMB::sanity()}} is reported in its own column but does not gate
comparability. It is a broader battery of warnings — a range longer than the
survey domain, a random-field SD collapsing to zero, a large gradient — that
flags a model worth thinking about rather than one whose likelihood is
meaningless; gating on it would quietly drop the best-fitting candidate from
the comparison. Read it, and treat a \code{sanity = FALSE} winner as a reason to
simplify rather than as a row to ignore.
}

\subsection{Cost}{

A cross-validated candidate costs \code{k} fits (plus one more when
\code{fit_full = TRUE}, which is needed for AIC, sanity and per-candidate indices).
Cross-validating a long ladder is therefore usually the wrong order of work:
rank all candidates by AIC first (\code{cv = FALSE}), then cross-validate a
shortlist taken from that ranking with \code{\link[=best_candidate]{best_candidate()}}. With \code{cache_dir}
set and the same \code{cache_prefix}, the second call reuses the whole-frame fits
from the first and pays only for the folds.
}

\subsection{Caching and resumability}{

\code{cache_dir} writes \strong{two files per candidate} — the whole-frame fit and the
cross-validation separately — so an interrupted run resumes instead of
starting over, and a cross-validated call under the same \code{cache_prefix}
reuses the fits an earlier AIC ladder already paid for instead of repeating
them. \code{only} restricts a call to named candidates so a long ladder can be
spread over several R sessions (a large spatiotemporal fit can exhaust memory
when a dozen of them accumulate in one process).
}
